 ## Background
-The technique was popularized in the early 2000s as graphics hardware gained support for 3D textures and fragment shaders. Key references include Lefohn et al. (2004) for on-the-fly slice reconstruction, Rezk-Salama et al. (2000) for trilinear interpolation in fragment shaders, and Kniss et al. (2002b) for addressing intensity variations and image-popping artifacts [S1]. It is widely used in medical imaging and scientific visualization where real-time interaction is required on older or constrained hardware [S1].
+The method was introduced in the early 2000s as a means to leverage the fixed-function pipeline of graphics hardware for volume rendering. Key developments include the use of 3D textures and proxy geometry for view-aligned slicing [S2, unofficial][S6]. The approach was further refined to handle shadows and translucency using two-pass techniques [S2, unofficial]. It is widely used in medical imaging and scientific visualization where interactive frame rates are required on standard graphics hardware.
 
